    "lawCode": "Add §9-aa, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9656\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the banking law, in relation to prohibiting fees for\nelectronic benefit transfer services\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection One: Adds a new section 9-aa to the Banking Law prohibiting\nbanks and ATM operators from charging consumers or benefit recipients\nsurcharges or fees for using EBT cards, without disrupting \"backend\"\ninterbank and network fees. This section also excludes federally char-\ntered institutions, includes a severability clause, and authorizes the\nDepartment of Financial Services, in consultation with the Office of\nTemporary and Disability Assistance, to promulgate regulations needed to\nimplement this bill.\n\nSection Two: Effective Date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs of May 2020, New York State had paid out $6.8 billion in unemployment\ninsurance benefits to the more than 1.8 million New Yorkers who had\nfiled for unemployment benefits since the beginning of the COVID-19\npandemic in March 2020. Even as New York State begins a phased reopening\nof its regional economies, the total benefits paid and unemployment\ninsurance (UI) claimants will continue to grow for the foreseeable\nfuture.\n\nNew York UI claimants have the option of receiving their UI benefits on\na debit card issued by KeyBank, which currently holds a contract with\nNew York, State to administer benefit banking services for Ul, as well\nas the Department of Labor's Occupational Training Act program and the\nOffice of Children & Family Services' Adoption Subsidy Electronic\nPayments program. According to a June 2020 New York Times article, about\nhalf a million New Yorkers receive their UI benefits via the KeyBank\ndebit cards.\n\nKeyBank charges benefit recipients using their debit cards a $1.50 per\nwithdrawal fee at domestic ATMs outside of the KeyBank ATM network.  For\nNew Yorkers who have lost their jobs due to COVID-19-related\nrestrictions on their place of employment, UI benefits are a lifeline.\nBanks holding lucrative contracts with New York State to provide banking\nservices for the administration of public benefits should not be eating\ninto recipients' benefits with fees for basic transactions like ATM\nwithdrawals. This bill would prohibit any bank providing benefit banking\nservices to New York State or its local governments from charging fees\nto benefit recipients.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n(2024) A.8999-C Tapia: Vetoed Memo.129\n\n(2023) A.8999 Tapia: Referred to Banks\n\n(2022) A.5733 Joyner: Referred to Banks\n\n(2021) A.5733 Joyner: Referrdd to Banks\n\n(2020) A.10670 Joyner: Introduced to Banks\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law and shall apply to any contract for electronic\nbenefit transfer services as defined in section 9-aa of the banking law,\nas added by section one of this act, entered into on or after such\neffective date between the state, or any agency, subdivision or depart-\nment thereof, and any entity subject to the New York state banking law,\ngeneral business law, or financial services law.\n",
